#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101975 :tutorial/content "Multiplying a binomial by itself $n$ times is known as a **binomial expansion**.\nFor instance, multiplying the binomial $(a + b)$ by itself gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nLet's consider the binomial expansion of\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTo write out the expansion, we use two patterns to simplify most of the hard work: the **exponent pattern** and the **coefficient pattern**.\n\n- The **exponent pattern** is\n$a^{\\color{red}n}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad a^{\\color{red}{n-1}}b^{{\\color{blue}1}}, \\quad a^{{\\color{red}n-2}}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dots\\quad a^{\\color{red}2}b^{{\\color{blue}n-2}},\\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{{\\color{blue}n - 1}}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}0}b^{{\\color{blue}n}}$.\nThe exponents of $a$ start at $n$ and go down ($n, n-1, \\cdots, 1, 0$) while the exponents of $b$ increase from $0$ to $n$. In every term, the two exponents always add up to $n$.\n- The **coefficient pattern** can be found using Pascal's Triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 5 & 1 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮$\nWe take the top row to be the $0$th row. Then, the $n$th row of Pascal's Triangle are the coefficients of $(a+b)^n$.\n\nLet's use this to expand the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^3\n$$\n\nThe exponent pattern is\n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nSince $n=3$, the coefficient pattern is found using the $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle: $(1,3,3,1)$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3 = \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2} + \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the above gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3= a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3}\n$$\n\nand we're done!" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a75ca66-5c0a-578d-ad4a-170fe5297413" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3385 :tutorial/title "Expanding Binomials Using Pascal’s Triangle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101977 :tutorial/content "One interesting number pattern is **Pascal's triangle**, named after Blaise Pascal, a famous French mathematician.\n\nPascal's triangle is made by rows of numbers that form a triangle.\nEach row has one more number than the row above.\nThe first two rows are made of three $1$s in a triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nLet's construct the next row.\nThe first and last number in each row is always $1$.\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}◻\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nThe other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are ${\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{blue}1}$.\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $\\color{blue}1$ and ${\\color{blue}1}$:\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = {\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we have the first three rows of Pascal's triangle.\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nIf we continue repeating this process for each row, we will get the following results for the first six rows of Pascal's triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 5 & 1 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f708f697-814a-58cd-8926-d278c8e15fc4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3392 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101990 :tutorial/content "The following table shows the first six binomial expansions.\n\n| **Power** | **Pascal's Triangle** | **Binomial** | **Binomial Expansion** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^1$ | $\\mathbf{1}a + \\mathbf{1}b$ |\n| $2$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{2}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^2$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^2 + \\mathbf{2}ab + \\mathbf{1}b^2$ |\n| $3$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^3$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^3 + \\mathbf{3}a^2b + \\mathbf{3}ab^2 + \\mathbf{1}b^3$ |\n| $4$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{6}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^4$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4$ |\n| $5$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\!\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^5$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^5 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}a^4b \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^3b^2 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^2b^3 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}ab^4 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{1}b^5$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "249c4559-4588-59e9-ae28-c70bc71b66d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3895 :tutorial/title "Table of Results"}]}
